1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
If you think wood is just about 2x4s in panel, think again.

2
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:17,000
It holds up entire cities, feeds our passion for barbecuing,

3
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:21,000
and inspires good old American grit and ingenuity.

4
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:25,000
It's a beast to hold on to. It throws you around like a rag doll.

5
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:30,740
From giving shelter to taking wing,

6
00:00:31,240 --> 00:00:35,520
this is one material that can do it all.

7
00:00:36,040 --> 00:00:38,280
It's a modern marvel because it's just so versatile.

8
00:00:38,900 --> 00:00:41,920
We may think we live in the age of plastic and steel,

9
00:00:42,540 --> 00:00:45,580
but America is still built on wood.

10
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:05,320
Any way you cut it, we need wood.

11
00:01:05,980 --> 00:01:09,480
Worldwide, we use about 4 billion tons of wood a year,

12
00:01:09,840 --> 00:01:13,800
and that amounts to about 3 1⁄2 pounds of wood per person per day.

13
00:01:14,360 --> 00:01:19,260
A lot of the times we use wood, and we don't even think of it as wood.

14
00:01:19,700 --> 00:01:23,040
Whether it's making a $3 million Stradivarius singh

15
00:01:23,041 --> 00:01:25,240
or putting pencil to paper.

16
00:01:26,700 --> 00:01:28,040
Wood can do it all.

17
00:01:30,320 --> 00:01:32,540
It can even be twice as strong as steel,

18
00:01:32,860 --> 00:01:34,800
pound for pound, when it's used the right way.

19
00:01:35,820 --> 00:01:39,280
Wood pilings are long posts driven deep underground

20
00:01:39,281 --> 00:01:41,860
that can support 35 times their weight.

21
00:01:42,240 --> 00:01:44,140
They hold up beach houses in Malibu,

22
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:46,980
the Brooklyn Bridge,

23
00:01:48,060 --> 00:01:48,920
the Superdome,

24
00:01:49,380 --> 00:01:51,320
and even the city of Venice.

25
00:01:51,321 --> 00:01:55,320
The entire city of Venice is supported by wood pilings.

26
00:01:55,420 --> 00:01:57,960
St. Mark's Cathedral is estimated that it's supported

27
00:01:58,060 --> 00:02:02,060
by about a million wood pilings that were pounded into the ground

28
00:02:02,160 --> 00:02:05,560
one by one to firm up the soggy soils of Venice.

29
00:02:05,660 --> 00:02:08,500
The pilings of Venice are about 1,000 years old.

30
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:10,040
They're still there today.

31
00:02:10,140 --> 00:02:12,980
They're still there doing their job.

32
00:02:13,080 --> 00:02:14,980
In the last 1,000 years,

33
00:02:15,080 --> 00:02:17,020
the process of making wood pilings

34
00:02:17,120 --> 00:02:20,620
and how quickly we can do it has been revolutionized

35
00:02:20,621 --> 00:02:23,620
I would say probably in the last three weeks,

36
00:02:23,720 --> 00:02:26,020
these logs were still standing as trees.

37
00:02:26,120 --> 00:02:29,020
First, not just any tree can become a pilot.

38
00:02:33,960 --> 00:02:38,280
Trees in the forest, there's only 10 trees per acre

39
00:02:38,380 --> 00:02:41,280
that will be considered a polar pilot quality.

40
00:02:43,480 --> 00:02:47,420
Only the longest, straightest trees make the cut.

41
00:02:47,520 --> 00:02:49,780
At Cassanche Treating in Louisiana,

42
00:02:49,781 --> 00:02:55,113
Louisiana. Yellow pine trees arrive, stripped of their branches.

43
00:02:55,114 --> 00:02:57,780
One of the first processes we do

44
00:02:57,781 --> 00:03:03,263
is remove the bark from the tree. This is the pole filler.

45
00:03:03,264 --> 00:03:06,760
All that season does is just tear the

46
00:03:06,761 --> 00:03:10,340
bark off the pole. It's loud, it's mean machine.

47
00:03:10,341 --> 00:03:13,920
Cutting heads deliver a one-two punch. The first

48
00:03:13,921 --> 00:03:16,879
has coarser blades to chew through the thick outer bark.

49
00:03:16,880 --> 00:03:18,940
The second takes off what's left of the

50
00:03:18,941 --> 00:03:23,940
On average, we'll peel about 300 to 400 poles a day.

51
00:03:24,040 --> 00:03:26,940
Once peeled, the log is graded based on length

52
00:03:27,040 --> 00:03:27,940
and circumference.

53
00:03:28,040 --> 00:03:31,480
They cut the pole to the nearest multiple of five feet

54
00:03:31,580 --> 00:03:33,680
to standardize the lengths.

55
00:03:33,780 --> 00:03:37,200
Pilings start out at about 100% moisture level.

56
00:03:37,300 --> 00:03:38,900
Before they can add preservative,

57
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:41,800
Kasachie needs to cook off some of that liquid.

58
00:03:41,900 --> 00:03:43,740
They move the pilings into a kiln,

59
00:03:43,840 --> 00:03:48,940
where they'll be heated to 225 degrees for 72 hours.

60
00:03:48,941 --> 00:03:50,740
It's like a sauna for wood.

61
00:03:50,840 --> 00:03:53,780
That's enough to boil the moisture out of the piling.

62
00:03:53,880 --> 00:03:57,880
Our target moisture is 23% to 27% of the piling.

63
00:03:57,980 --> 00:03:59,380
That's one quarter of the piling's

64
00:03:59,480 --> 00:04:00,680
original moisture content.

65
00:04:03,580 --> 00:04:05,560
What happens next is a step that has done more

66
00:04:05,660 --> 00:04:09,420
to help preserve wood than anything before.

67
00:04:09,520 --> 00:04:10,900
Pressure treatment.

68
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:14,500
Under pressure, preservative will be forced into the dry pilings,

69
00:04:14,600 --> 00:04:17,100
where it will fight rot and insects.

70
00:04:17,101 --> 00:04:19,800
Kassanshi has North America's largest pressure chamber

71
00:04:19,801 --> 00:04:22,960
at 150 feet long and 8 feet wide

72
00:04:22,961 --> 00:04:25,400
and with 1 1⁄4-inch thick walls.

73
00:04:26,360 --> 00:04:29,160
A big chamber means big chemical tanks.

74
00:04:29,440 --> 00:04:32,700
What you're looking at now is our preservative tanks.

75
00:04:32,880 --> 00:04:35,400
They'll hold approximately 57,000 gallons.

76
00:04:36,080 --> 00:04:38,400
Once we get the pylons into this cylinder,

77
00:04:38,900 --> 00:04:41,500
we'll dump all the preservative into this cylinder.

78
00:04:42,660 --> 00:04:45,300
This wood, it'll just suck that chemical into it.

79
00:04:45,480 --> 00:04:46,980
After sweating in the kiln,

80
00:04:47,100 --> 00:04:49,660
The pilings are like squeezed sponges,

81
00:04:49,661 --> 00:04:51,180
so they'll suck up the preservative

82
00:04:51,181 --> 00:04:54,300
and return to the 100% moisture level.

83
00:04:54,301 --> 00:04:58,540
Pressure will force the preservative in faster and deeper.

84
00:04:58,541 --> 00:05:00,220
All right, we got the wood in here.

85
00:05:00,221 --> 00:05:01,980
We got the process started.

86
00:05:01,981 --> 00:05:04,140
It's going to take about two, two and a half hours

87
00:05:04,141 --> 00:05:06,060
to get it complete.

88
00:05:06,061 --> 00:05:08,220
First, they create a vacuum in the chamber

89
00:05:08,221 --> 00:05:10,620
for 30 minutes to open the wood fibers.

90
00:05:10,621 --> 00:05:13,020
Then they add the chemical and raise the pressure

91
00:05:13,021 --> 00:05:16,380
to 150 PSI for 15 minutes.

92
00:05:16,381 --> 00:05:18,620
This forces the preservative deep into the wood.

93
00:05:20,120 --> 00:05:22,780
Then they pull a final vacuum for an hour

94
00:05:22,781 --> 00:05:24,480
to draw out any extra fluid.

95
00:05:25,740 --> 00:05:28,160
When the piling comes out, it'll be dry to the touch.

96
00:05:29,100 --> 00:05:30,280
Infused with preservative,

97
00:05:30,940 --> 00:05:33,400
the wood is ready for shipment.

98
00:05:35,100 --> 00:05:37,260
This load is part of a much bigger order

99
00:05:37,261 --> 00:05:38,460
heading to New Orleans.

100
00:05:41,820 --> 00:05:44,120
There, the pilings will turn soft ground

101
00:05:44,121 --> 00:05:45,640
into a school's foundation.

102
00:05:46,380 --> 00:05:48,780
To the common eye, this ground looks hard,

103
00:05:48,781 --> 00:05:51,180
but when you start putting a lot of weight on top of it,

104
00:05:51,181 --> 00:05:52,580
it won't support it.

105
00:05:52,581 --> 00:05:55,920
It's a silty soil condition here.

106
00:05:55,921 --> 00:05:58,320
To create a stable foundation,

107
00:05:58,321 --> 00:06:01,360
they'll drive pilings 40 feet down to a solid layer

108
00:06:01,361 --> 00:06:03,760
known as the hard pad.

109
00:06:03,761 --> 00:06:05,700
The pilings will act like stilts,

110
00:06:05,701 --> 00:06:08,000
supporting whatever is built on top of them.

111
00:06:08,001 --> 00:06:11,040
Each one of these poles hold approximately 35 tons,

112
00:06:11,041 --> 00:06:13,500
and the stress load that you're gonna use on top of that,

113
00:06:13,501 --> 00:06:16,360
It's a fraction compared to what these things can hold.

114
00:06:17,340 --> 00:06:23,760
The muscle of the operation is a modified crane known as a pile driver, like the wrestling move named after it.

115
00:06:26,600 --> 00:06:29,700
The pile driver isn't pretty, but it gets results.

116
00:06:30,460 --> 00:06:33,800
First, the crew raises a one-ton piling into position.

117
00:06:34,340 --> 00:06:36,340
They're going to go and pick up the butt of the pole.

118
00:06:36,700 --> 00:06:39,200
They're going to pull it all the way up to the top of the pile driver.

119
00:06:43,500 --> 00:06:47,500
Then the crew has to create a guide hole.

120
00:06:47,501 --> 00:06:50,500
They'll do a process called water jetting.

121
00:06:50,501 --> 00:06:54,500
And what this does is when you get in a silty or a sandy condition,

122
00:06:54,501 --> 00:06:58,500
the water jetting just displaces the dirt for a moment.

123
00:06:58,501 --> 00:07:02,500
They've got to drop the piling before the dirt rushes back into the hole.

124
00:07:02,501 --> 00:07:06,500
The cage acts like a guide or sled.

125
00:07:06,501 --> 00:07:08,500
It's going to fall within the cage.

126
00:07:08,501 --> 00:07:11,500
They're going to drop this hammer hard.

127
00:07:13,500 --> 00:07:18,500
Once the pilings in the hole, they pound it deeper.

128
00:07:18,501 --> 00:07:24,500
This is a drop hammer, and you know, the force that's being generated by the drop hammer,

129
00:07:24,501 --> 00:07:27,500
depending on the weights that they have on it, is tremendous.

130
00:07:27,501 --> 00:07:30,500
Each blow delivers a force of over two tons.

131
00:07:30,501 --> 00:07:36,500
This big white box on the back of the crane is going to generate the compression to pull the hammer up,

132
00:07:36,501 --> 00:07:40,500
and then literally gravity itself is going to do the rest.

133
00:07:43,500 --> 00:07:45,400
The hammer ball is gonna drop,

134
00:07:45,401 --> 00:07:47,740
and it's gonna hit the top of the pole,

135
00:07:47,741 --> 00:07:50,040
and there you have it, you're driving a pile.

136
00:07:57,680 --> 00:07:59,480
Hard hats are required,

137
00:07:59,481 --> 00:08:01,860
and another piece of gear is highly recommended.

138
00:08:01,861 --> 00:08:04,260
It's helpful to wear earplugs all the time out here,

139
00:08:04,261 --> 00:08:06,060
you can tell it's pretty loud.

140
00:08:06,061 --> 00:08:08,400
Probably close to 180 decibels.

141
00:08:08,401 --> 00:08:12,120
180 decibels is as loud as a stun grenade,

142
00:08:12,121 --> 00:08:14,520
and louder than a heavy metal concert.

143
00:08:14,521 --> 00:08:16,020
They know they've hit the hard pan

144
00:08:16,021 --> 00:08:18,560
when they pound the piling 22 times,

145
00:08:18,561 --> 00:08:20,860
and it moves less than a foot.

146
00:08:20,861 --> 00:08:22,960
The pilings compact the dirt around them,

147
00:08:22,961 --> 00:08:25,600
creating friction that keeps them in place.

148
00:08:25,601 --> 00:08:27,600
And that's the reason why you come over here,

149
00:08:27,601 --> 00:08:30,300
and you shake these poles, you can't even get them to budge.

150
00:08:30,301 --> 00:08:32,300
I mean, this stuff sets up like concrete

151
00:08:32,301 --> 00:08:34,440
once the pole's driven.

152
00:08:34,441 --> 00:08:37,040
The crew can drive 40 to 50 piles a day,

153
00:08:37,041 --> 00:08:40,680
so it'll take about a month to set the base for a foundation.

154
00:08:40,681 --> 00:08:43,100
After they drive all 1,000 pilings,

155
00:08:43,340 --> 00:08:45,020
they'll cut the tops to the same height,

156
00:08:45,280 --> 00:08:46,920
connect them with steel and cement beams,

157
00:08:47,220 --> 00:08:49,360
and then pour the concrete slab on top.

158
00:08:50,120 --> 00:08:52,240
This will be the new school's foundation.

159
00:08:52,840 --> 00:08:56,120
The school will eventually weigh 25,000 tons,

160
00:08:56,540 --> 00:08:58,300
the equivalent of two cargo ships,

161
00:08:58,520 --> 00:09:01,720
and every single pound of it will rest on the timber pilings.

162
00:09:02,680 --> 00:09:05,560
And the school is just one of hundreds of buildings in the area

163
00:09:05,561 --> 00:09:07,340
that need wood to stay above the mud.

164
00:09:07,720 --> 00:09:10,200
This city is built on friction piling.

165
00:09:10,680 --> 00:09:13,380
Pretty much New Orleans is built on stilts.

166
00:09:13,381 --> 00:09:16,520
Both New Orleans and Venice are waterlogged,

167
00:09:16,521 --> 00:09:18,980
which seems like the worst places for wood.

168
00:09:18,981 --> 00:09:21,420
But actually, the opposite is true.

169
00:09:21,421 --> 00:09:24,760
Why? Because wood doesn't rot underwater.

170
00:09:24,761 --> 00:09:26,760
Once it's driven below the water table,

171
00:09:26,761 --> 00:09:28,440
just below the surface of the earth,

172
00:09:28,441 --> 00:09:29,940
they're not gonna have any oxygen,

173
00:09:29,941 --> 00:09:32,600
and it takes oxygen for the rot

174
00:09:32,601 --> 00:09:35,540
and the different bug infestation to occur.

175
00:09:35,541 --> 00:09:37,100
So these things will never be touched.

176
00:09:37,101 --> 00:09:39,580
They'll be here hundreds of years from now.

177
00:09:39,581 --> 00:09:42,640
A lot of people think, you know, wood and water just don't mix.

178
00:09:42,760 --> 00:09:47,380
But the fact of the matter is, as long as wood stays constantly wet, it will last indefinitely.

179
00:09:48,040 --> 00:09:50,840
One of the more surprising uses of wood is as a water pipe.

180
00:09:51,600 --> 00:09:54,120
In England, they unearthed hundreds of miles of this stuff.

181
00:09:54,240 --> 00:09:55,060
It's in New York City.

182
00:09:55,600 --> 00:10:00,440
There's sections of Sedona, Arizona, where the water system is still made out of wood pipe.

183
00:10:00,441 --> 00:10:03,960
So as long as it stays wet, it'll last hundreds of years.

184
00:10:04,940 --> 00:10:08,780
Ever versatile, wood gives builders an excellent strength to weight ratio

185
00:10:08,781 --> 00:10:10,780
and won't rot underwater.

186
00:10:10,880 --> 00:10:13,080
But it also has a weakness,

187
00:10:13,180 --> 00:10:15,680
one that martial artists know how to exploit.

188
00:10:23,820 --> 00:10:29,040
A 2,000-pound wood pilot can support 35 tons.

189
00:10:29,140 --> 00:10:31,040
But even a child can smash a board.

190
00:10:31,140 --> 00:10:32,740
Yeah!

191
00:10:32,840 --> 00:10:35,840
How can wood be both so strong and so weak?

192
00:10:38,780 --> 00:10:41,380
Many martial artists know the answer,

193
00:10:41,480 --> 00:10:43,420
because breaking boards is a common way

194
00:10:43,520 --> 00:10:45,320
to teach correct striking technique.

195
00:10:49,460 --> 00:10:52,720
Breaking boards is comparable to breaking bones.

196
00:10:52,820 --> 00:10:54,720
If you can break a one-inch board,

197
00:10:54,820 --> 00:10:56,840
you can break a bone in the body.

198
00:10:56,940 --> 00:10:59,500
So students will practice breaking boards

199
00:10:59,600 --> 00:11:01,140
as opposed to breaking bones.

200
00:11:03,740 --> 00:11:05,740
But brute force won't split a board.

201
00:11:05,741 --> 00:11:09,740
You won't split a board.

202
00:11:09,741 --> 00:11:11,740
You have to hit it with the grain.

203
00:11:11,741 --> 00:11:13,740
How you align the board does make a difference

204
00:11:13,741 --> 00:11:15,740
in breaking it.

205
00:11:15,741 --> 00:11:17,740
You want to go with the grain.

206
00:11:17,741 --> 00:11:19,740
You don't want to go against the grain.

207
00:11:19,741 --> 00:11:21,740
So when you put the boards down, you want to make sure

208
00:11:21,741 --> 00:11:24,740
that they're all aligned together.

209
00:11:24,741 --> 00:11:27,740
Hit it the wrong way.

210
00:11:27,741 --> 00:11:30,740
And it's not the wood that breaks.

211
00:11:30,741 --> 00:11:32,740
I've seen people break their hands.

212
00:11:32,741 --> 00:11:34,740
What makes wood strong in one direction

213
00:11:34,741 --> 00:11:40,240
and weaken the other, starts with a tree.

214
00:11:40,241 --> 00:11:42,240
This is a wood factory.

215
00:11:42,241 --> 00:11:45,420
Under the bark, the tree's fibers are long and tough,

216
00:11:45,421 --> 00:11:47,680
like densely packed wires.

217
00:11:47,681 --> 00:11:51,060
And each year, the tree grows by adding a layer of these fibers,

218
00:11:51,061 --> 00:11:53,620
creating rings.

219
00:11:53,621 --> 00:11:56,620
If you look around a forest, you notice that trees are actually

220
00:11:56,621 --> 00:11:58,940
these strange-looking columns.

221
00:11:58,941 --> 00:12:00,840
They're actually pretty alien-looking.

222
00:12:00,841 --> 00:12:04,500
They're these big, long growths that are very tall and thin.

223
00:12:04,501 --> 00:12:08,900
and the force on them is almost entirely going down.

224
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:12,300
And so it makes sense that trees would have built themselves

225
00:12:12,400 --> 00:12:15,340
to be strong towards compression.

226
00:12:15,440 --> 00:12:17,340
You look at this thing and it is a column.

227
00:12:17,440 --> 00:12:18,440
That's what it is.

228
00:12:18,540 --> 00:12:20,440
And therefore, that's how we use it.

229
00:12:20,540 --> 00:12:21,560
We use it that way.

230
00:12:21,660 --> 00:12:26,160
Even when you're framing a house with joists and 2x4s,

231
00:12:26,260 --> 00:12:29,460
you're using the long direction of the 2x4 for the strength.

232
00:12:29,560 --> 00:12:32,560
But how do you take material that's strong in one direction

233
00:12:32,561 --> 00:12:37,160
strong in all directions, like a car wheel.

234
00:12:37,260 --> 00:12:40,960
Almost all early cars and more than 3 million of Henry Ford's

235
00:12:41,060 --> 00:12:45,300
Model Ts had wooden wheels.

236
00:12:45,400 --> 00:12:48,500
Many lasted longer than the cars themselves.

237
00:12:48,600 --> 00:12:51,220
But today, if you need a replacement wheel,

238
00:12:51,320 --> 00:12:55,720
you'll need it custom made by a specialist like Bill Calimer.

239
00:12:55,820 --> 00:12:58,720
He's one of the last people in the world who knows the secrets

240
00:12:58,820 --> 00:13:01,360
of making a wheel from wood.

241
00:13:01,361 --> 00:13:05,360
For the automobile wheels, I use hickory.

242
00:13:05,460 --> 00:13:10,000
Hickory is a very strong wood, and one of its major properties

243
00:13:10,100 --> 00:13:11,300
is it's bendable.

244
00:13:11,400 --> 00:13:13,800
It's very flexible for the spokes as well,

245
00:13:13,900 --> 00:13:17,600
and that allows the wheels to last a long time.

246
00:13:17,700 --> 00:13:20,720
Flexing, or bending, is the key to using wood strength

247
00:13:20,820 --> 00:13:23,220
to its fullest.

248
00:13:23,320 --> 00:13:25,760
The Model T spokes connect to a two-piece section

249
00:13:25,860 --> 00:13:28,720
of the inner rim.

250
00:13:28,820 --> 00:13:31,300
These wooden pieces have to bend without a grip

251
00:13:31,360 --> 00:13:33,960
the outer fibers, or they'll crack and break.

252
00:13:36,760 --> 00:13:40,160
Bill starts by cutting 1 1⁄2-inch wide pieces of hickory

253
00:13:40,260 --> 00:13:43,100
to form the two halves of the inner rim.

254
00:13:43,200 --> 00:13:46,400
If he tried bending them now, they'd break.

255
00:13:46,500 --> 00:13:49,920
So he prepares them for bending by putting them into a steamer.

256
00:13:50,020 --> 00:13:52,840
The steamer heats the wood to over 250 degrees

257
00:13:52,940 --> 00:13:54,620
and will loosen the fibers.

258
00:13:54,720 --> 00:13:56,660
The rule of thumb for steaming wood

259
00:13:56,760 --> 00:13:59,360
is that it should be in steam for an hour

260
00:13:59,361 --> 00:14:00,700
for an hour to the inch of thickness.

261
00:14:01,160 --> 00:14:03,460
And these are approximately an inch and a half thick,

262
00:14:03,780 --> 00:14:06,180
so they're going to be in there approximately an hour and a half.

263
00:14:06,820 --> 00:14:08,100
While the rim pieces steam,

264
00:14:08,880 --> 00:14:11,760
Bill makes the spokes, starting from a master template.

265
00:14:12,480 --> 00:14:14,260
Each car model has its own style.

266
00:14:14,800 --> 00:14:18,320
Up here I have primarily Model T Ford,

267
00:14:18,680 --> 00:14:20,580
some early Cadillac Stanley steamer.

268
00:14:20,581 --> 00:14:23,860
This shelf is primarily Buick, later Cadillac,

269
00:14:24,060 --> 00:14:26,940
Pierce Arrow, Oakland I see there.

270
00:14:26,941 --> 00:14:29,940
Using the template, he marks and then

271
00:14:30,040 --> 00:14:31,340
cuts out rough blanks.

272
00:14:35,240 --> 00:14:38,880
On an asymmetrical lathe, the template serves as the guide,

273
00:14:38,980 --> 00:14:41,080
and Bill can turn three new spokes at a time.

274
00:14:43,700 --> 00:14:46,800
After 90 minutes in the steamer, the rim pieces are ready.

275
00:14:46,900 --> 00:14:48,600
The heat has loosened the fibers enough

276
00:14:48,700 --> 00:14:50,700
that they can bend without breaking.

277
00:14:50,800 --> 00:14:53,500
But outer fibers could still get stressed in the bending,

278
00:14:53,600 --> 00:14:56,940
so Bill has made his own one-of-a-kind bending machine.

279
00:14:56,941 --> 00:15:01,140
It presses down on the center of the boards, pushes in from the

280
00:15:01,141 --> 00:15:06,180
ends, and keeps constant pressure on the outsides.

281
00:15:06,181 --> 00:15:10,180
The result? Compressed fibers in the inner section and fibers

282
00:15:10,181 --> 00:15:14,180
that haven't been stressed on the outside section.

283
00:15:14,181 --> 00:15:16,700
The rim pieces stay in the vending machine two to three

284
00:15:16,701 --> 00:15:21,700
hours until they cool so that they'll keep their shape.

285
00:15:21,701 --> 00:15:26,700
In the next step, Bill lays out the spokes.

286
00:15:26,701 --> 00:15:30,700
Once they fit together, he bolts them to a metal hub.

287
00:15:30,800 --> 00:15:33,700
This forms what's known as a spider.

288
00:15:33,800 --> 00:15:36,700
Now he has to line the spokes up exactly right with the two

289
00:15:36,800 --> 00:15:40,700
halves of the inner rim. It's very important to put the

290
00:15:40,800 --> 00:15:49,700
proper spoke in the proper hole. If they're bent correctly,

291
00:15:49,800 --> 00:15:53,700
they fit perfectly. It just takes a little TLC and a

292
00:15:53,701 --> 00:15:57,700
and a hammer to work them into an outer metal rim.

293
00:15:57,701 --> 00:16:00,700
Last step, check that it's true and balanced.

294
00:16:00,701 --> 00:16:02,700
This wheel's looking pretty good.

295
00:16:02,701 --> 00:16:03,700
It's running pretty straight.

296
00:16:03,701 --> 00:16:06,700
Now all I have to do is make three more.

297
00:16:06,701 --> 00:16:10,700
Bill builds wheels for roughly 100 cars each year.

298
00:16:10,701 --> 00:16:16,700
Ford churned out almost one million Model Ts in 1920 alone.

299
00:16:16,701 --> 00:16:18,700
That's at least four million wheels a year,

300
00:16:18,701 --> 00:16:21,700
which wasn't just a monumental feat.

301
00:16:21,701 --> 00:16:25,100
It also created mountains of wood scrap.

302
00:16:25,200 --> 00:16:27,940
Henry Ford hated waste, so he and a partner

303
00:16:28,040 --> 00:16:29,940
figured out a way to use all this scrap.

304
00:16:32,440 --> 00:16:35,940
They turned it into charcoal.

305
00:16:36,040 --> 00:16:38,760
Ford dealers sold it by the bag and would occasionally

306
00:16:38,860 --> 00:16:41,960
throw one in if you bought a car.

307
00:16:42,060 --> 00:16:46,060
Barbecuing became part of the American experience.

308
00:16:46,160 --> 00:16:49,860
In 1951, investors bought the charcoal business

309
00:16:49,861 --> 00:16:54,060
and name the new company after Ford's partner, E.J. Kingsford.

310
00:16:54,820 --> 00:16:58,160
Today, Kingsford is the world's largest producer of charcoal briquettes.

311
00:16:59,820 --> 00:17:01,820
Its biggest plant is in Bell, Missouri,

312
00:17:02,500 --> 00:17:07,000
where they use the same technique and some of the same equipment as Ford once used.

313
00:17:09,280 --> 00:17:12,500
I'm standing at the beginning of the charcoal briquette making process.

314
00:17:12,501 --> 00:17:16,120
This is a wood mountain, basically. It's our hog fuel pile.

315
00:17:16,121 --> 00:17:20,020
The hog fuel pile is about 40,000 tons of wood

316
00:17:20,021 --> 00:17:21,160
from local sawmills,

317
00:17:21,660 --> 00:17:24,320
enough to keep the plant running 24 hours a day,

318
00:17:24,520 --> 00:17:25,500
seven days a week.

319
00:17:27,300 --> 00:17:30,960
Kingsford consumes about 250,000 tons of it a year

320
00:17:30,961 --> 00:17:31,980
at this plant alone,

321
00:17:32,200 --> 00:17:34,180
and over a million tons company-wide.

322
00:17:35,780 --> 00:17:37,500
They process so much wood,

323
00:17:37,940 --> 00:17:40,080
it takes a bulldozer to load the conveyor belt.

324
00:17:41,760 --> 00:17:44,780
The first step is to dry the wood in heated drums.

325
00:17:44,781 --> 00:17:49,820
We're changing the wood from about 50% moisture down to about 5.

326
00:17:49,821 --> 00:17:55,320
From the dryer, the wood is then run through a five-story oven known as a retort furnace.

327
00:17:55,321 --> 00:17:57,740
This is where wood becomes charcoal.

328
00:17:57,741 --> 00:18:01,480
The retort furnace itself is really the heart of the char-making unit.

329
00:18:01,481 --> 00:18:05,440
The key to making charcoal is superheating the wood without burning it.

330
00:18:05,441 --> 00:18:10,720
In a low-oxygen environment, the dried wood travels through five levels, with temperatures

331
00:18:10,721 --> 00:18:14,280
reaching 1,200 degrees.

332
00:18:14,281 --> 00:18:16,280
to run the wood through the furnace.

333
00:18:16,281 --> 00:18:19,280
It converts about eight pounds of hog fuel

334
00:18:19,281 --> 00:18:21,280
into one pound of charcoal.

335
00:18:21,281 --> 00:18:23,280
At this point, it's just a powder.

336
00:18:23,281 --> 00:18:27,280
To make their trademark briquette, Kingsford binds the powder

337
00:18:27,281 --> 00:18:29,280
with other secret ingredients.

338
00:18:29,281 --> 00:18:32,280
We're at the discharge end of the presses,

339
00:18:32,281 --> 00:18:36,280
and the briquettes are coming out with the keg pressed in them.

340
00:18:36,281 --> 00:18:39,280
At this stage, the briquettes are more like brownies.

341
00:18:39,281 --> 00:18:42,280
So the next stage, we're gonna send them over to the dryers

342
00:18:42,281 --> 00:18:46,820
over to the dryers and dry them down to about 5% moisture.

343
00:18:46,821 --> 00:18:50,020
Along the way, the briquettes go through a shaker

344
00:18:50,021 --> 00:18:52,860
that takes off the rough edges.

345
00:18:52,861 --> 00:18:56,420
Then they're sorted into one of three dryers.

346
00:18:56,421 --> 00:18:58,020
We have three briquette dryers.

347
00:18:58,021 --> 00:19:01,760
Actually, two of them came down from Fort Charcoal in Michigan.

348
00:19:01,761 --> 00:19:04,040
So they're some of our oldest piece of equipment.

349
00:19:04,041 --> 00:19:05,440
By the time they get to the end of the dryer,

350
00:19:05,441 --> 00:19:07,340
they're now 5% moisture.

351
00:19:07,341 --> 00:19:12,240
They're dry, rock hard, and they're ready to send a package.

352
00:19:12,241 --> 00:19:15,240
Each 20-pound bag holds at least 300 briquettes.

353
00:19:15,241 --> 00:19:18,240
This is our distribution center at the Bell Plant.

354
00:19:18,241 --> 00:19:21,240
If you recall, we started at the wood mountain or wood pile.

355
00:19:21,241 --> 00:19:25,240
This is the last stop before we send them off to the customer.

356
00:19:25,241 --> 00:19:28,240
We have about 200,000 square feet of warehouse space here,

357
00:19:28,241 --> 00:19:30,240
and that's still not enough.

358
00:19:30,241 --> 00:19:34,240
A lot of it's going right to the customer.

359
00:19:34,241 --> 00:19:38,240
We'll probably see a couple hundred trucks in and out each day.

360
00:19:38,241 --> 00:19:42,240
Kingsford sells more than 1 billion pounds of charcoal every year.

361
00:19:42,241 --> 00:19:46,740
Fueling an American tradition, all thanks to Henry Ford's hatred of waste.

362
00:19:48,680 --> 00:19:50,720
But Ford didn't invent charcoal.

363
00:19:51,160 --> 00:19:53,100
Humans have been making it for thousands of years.

364
00:19:54,140 --> 00:19:56,580
It was a crucial fuel for many cultures.

365
00:19:58,100 --> 00:20:00,380
Charcoal can burn at a much higher temperature,

366
00:20:00,800 --> 00:20:02,820
and when you can get higher temperatures going,

367
00:20:03,040 --> 00:20:07,360
you can use that to smelt metals, to smelt glass, to smelt copper.

368
00:20:07,860 --> 00:20:10,880
A lot of people think that some of the downfalls of civilization

369
00:20:10,881 --> 00:20:13,640
were based on the fact that some of these civilizations

370
00:20:13,641 --> 00:20:14,520
ran out of wood.

371
00:20:15,020 --> 00:20:18,080
Vast tracts of forest were cut and burned to make charcoal.

372
00:20:18,720 --> 00:20:22,100
And when they ran out, they looked overseas for more.

373
00:20:25,620 --> 00:20:28,420
Wood got us off our feet and on a roll.

374
00:20:29,800 --> 00:20:33,340
The neat thing about wood is that it really allowed man

375
00:20:33,341 --> 00:20:35,460
to branch out all across the world,

376
00:20:35,800 --> 00:20:38,680
you know, to get from where mankind was going.

377
00:20:38,860 --> 00:20:40,760
If you were in a ship, it was made of wood.

378
00:20:40,880 --> 00:20:43,080
If it was on a ski, it was made of wood.

379
00:20:43,180 --> 00:20:44,920
If it was a wheel, it was made out of wood.

380
00:20:47,020 --> 00:20:50,300
Wood was how we explored and why we fought.

381
00:20:51,800 --> 00:20:55,100
The British Navy ruled the waves in the 1700s,

382
00:20:55,200 --> 00:20:58,560
but it needed a steady supply of tall, strong trees

383
00:20:58,561 --> 00:20:59,960
for its ship's masts.

384
00:21:00,940 --> 00:21:03,640
White pines in the New England colonies were ideal,

385
00:21:04,480 --> 00:21:07,020
and so the British crown claimed the largest for itself,

386
00:21:07,680 --> 00:21:09,160
even ones on private land.

387
00:21:10,880 --> 00:21:15,320
Angered by this policy, in 1772, colonists fought back

388
00:21:15,420 --> 00:21:18,020
in what became known as the Pine Tree Riot.

389
00:21:23,220 --> 00:21:26,220
Revolutionaries adopted the pine tree as a symbol,

390
00:21:26,320 --> 00:21:28,140
and it was the emblem on their flag

391
00:21:28,240 --> 00:21:29,640
at the Battle of Bunker Hill.

392
00:21:32,440 --> 00:21:35,140
Wood helped launch the nation, and it gave rise

393
00:21:35,240 --> 00:21:38,040
to an American industry.

394
00:21:38,041 --> 00:21:43,040
Lumberjacks harvested America's forests until the Second World War.

395
00:21:43,041 --> 00:21:46,040
Three, two, one, go!

396
00:21:46,041 --> 00:21:49,040
Today, their traditions live on as competitions,

397
00:21:49,041 --> 00:21:53,040
like the Johnny Appleseed Festival in Sheffield, Pennsylvania.

398
00:21:57,040 --> 00:21:59,040
These people love their wood.

399
00:21:59,041 --> 00:22:03,040
I'm Holly Waterfield, and I wear wooden shoes.

400
00:22:03,041 --> 00:22:08,040
We typically cut softwood, so we're cutting aspen.

401
00:22:08,041 --> 00:22:11,040
It's been under wet hay for a few months,

402
00:22:11,041 --> 00:22:13,040
and that helps it to even out.

403
00:22:15,040 --> 00:22:17,040
We want it to have moisture in it.

404
00:22:17,041 --> 00:22:20,040
Dry wood is a lot harder to cut,

405
00:22:20,041 --> 00:22:23,040
and with the axes that we're using, they're very thin-edged,

406
00:22:23,041 --> 00:22:26,040
so dry wood has a tendency to damage and dull the edge,

407
00:22:26,041 --> 00:22:30,040
so we have soft, moist wood.

408
00:22:33,040 --> 00:22:37,040
All the events come directly from skills the original

409
00:22:37,041 --> 00:22:39,040
lumberjacks needed.

410
00:22:39,041 --> 00:22:42,040
The springboard event dates back to when western lumberjacks

411
00:22:42,041 --> 00:22:45,040
had to make their own steps in the base of a tree to climb

412
00:22:45,041 --> 00:22:47,040
above its roots.

413
00:22:47,041 --> 00:22:49,040
To prep for that event, competitors clear away the

414
00:22:49,041 --> 00:22:52,040
bark and dirt so it won't damage their axes.

415
00:22:52,041 --> 00:22:56,040
Basically this is going to be my bottom pocket for my

416
00:22:56,041 --> 00:22:57,040
first board.

417
00:22:57,041 --> 00:23:00,040
3, 2, 1, go!

418
00:23:03,040 --> 00:23:08,040
And then once you finish, you gotta throw your axe up here.

419
00:23:08,041 --> 00:23:11,040
And then you're gonna throw your board in here.

420
00:23:11,041 --> 00:23:17,040
And you can grab onto the handle of that axe and use that as leverage to get up on your boards.

421
00:23:17,041 --> 00:23:20,040
This takes a little bit of everything.

422
00:23:20,041 --> 00:23:22,040
You gotta be balanced to stay on your board.

423
00:23:22,041 --> 00:23:26,040
And your board's kinda springing, you know, it's kinda bouncing while you're up there chomping.

424
00:23:26,041 --> 00:23:30,040
So it takes a lot of strength and endurance to be able to complete the whole thing.

425
00:23:33,040 --> 00:23:41,040
The underhand chop and the cross saw were used to cut down

426
00:23:41,041 --> 00:23:43,040
trees and divide them into logs.

427
00:23:46,040 --> 00:23:50,040
And log rollers helped keep huge stands of cut trees moving

428
00:23:50,041 --> 00:23:51,040
downriver.

429
00:23:51,041 --> 00:23:53,040
It was a dangerous business.

430
00:23:53,041 --> 00:23:56,040
One slip and you could be crushed between the logs.

431
00:23:56,041 --> 00:23:59,040
They say that for every one man that died in the woods cutting

432
00:23:59,041 --> 00:24:02,040
down trees, there were 10 that got killed on the river drives.

433
00:24:02,041 --> 00:24:04,980
So staying on top of the log was very essential

434
00:24:05,080 --> 00:24:06,980
to staying alive on a river drive.

435
00:24:07,080 --> 00:24:09,380
Thousands of logs were driven downriver.

436
00:24:09,480 --> 00:24:11,380
And if one got stuck on a rock,

437
00:24:11,480 --> 00:24:14,380
it could all pile up in a matter of minutes.

438
00:24:14,480 --> 00:24:17,160
It was a tangled, dangerous mess that gave rise

439
00:24:17,260 --> 00:24:19,060
to the term log jam.

440
00:24:19,160 --> 00:24:21,160
And then a couple, two or three lumberjacks,

441
00:24:21,260 --> 00:24:24,700
sometimes more, would have to go in and start removing logs

442
00:24:24,800 --> 00:24:27,640
at the bottom of the pile to break that log jam

443
00:24:27,740 --> 00:24:29,040
and get the logs rolling.

444
00:24:29,140 --> 00:24:31,000
And that is when a lot of them met their demise

445
00:24:31,001 --> 00:24:33,500
because oftentimes the whole wall would come down

446
00:24:33,501 --> 00:24:37,500
at one time and bury several of them.

447
00:24:37,501 --> 00:24:39,500
This is a Peg and Raker saw.

448
00:24:39,501 --> 00:24:41,000
So these are the teeth here.

449
00:24:41,001 --> 00:24:44,000
This would be the exact same thing they used in the woods.

450
00:24:44,001 --> 00:24:47,000
They're going through a 17-inch log in about seven seconds.

451
00:24:51,000 --> 00:24:53,000
But in lumberjack competitions,

452
00:24:53,001 --> 00:24:56,000
there's one machine you never see in the woods,

453
00:24:56,001 --> 00:24:58,000
chainsaws with engines built

454
00:24:58,001 --> 00:25:00,000
for much heavier loads than cutting.

455
00:25:00,001 --> 00:25:04,000
It came out of a 1996 Ski-Doo snowmobile.

456
00:25:04,001 --> 00:25:07,000
It's approximately 70 horsepower on paper.

457
00:25:07,001 --> 00:25:10,000
Turns the chain right around 200 miles an hour.

458
00:25:10,001 --> 00:25:11,000
It's a beast to hold onto.

459
00:25:11,001 --> 00:25:13,000
It throws you around like a rag doll.

460
00:25:15,000 --> 00:25:16,000
17-inch log.

461
00:25:16,001 --> 00:25:20,000
I should be right around 5'4", 5'5".

462
00:25:20,001 --> 00:25:22,000
Three cuts and starting it.

463
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:25,000
You make it go fast.

464
00:25:25,001 --> 00:25:28,000
This is as close as you can get to pure drag racing as there is.

465
00:25:28,001 --> 00:25:30,000
It's just you, the wood, and the motor,

466
00:25:30,001 --> 00:25:32,000
and a whole bunch of brand.

467
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:38,000
The original lumberjacks cut down trees

468
00:25:38,001 --> 00:25:40,000
that were hundreds of years old

469
00:25:40,001 --> 00:25:43,000
and that had wood that was straight and strong.

470
00:25:43,001 --> 00:25:45,000
It was so versatile,

471
00:25:45,001 --> 00:25:47,000
it became the country's building material,

472
00:25:47,001 --> 00:25:53,000
from railroad bridges to ornate mansions.

473
00:25:53,001 --> 00:25:55,000
Where is it today?

474
00:25:55,001 --> 00:25:57,000
Some of it is hiding beneath the beat-up experience

475
00:25:57,001 --> 00:26:00,040
the beat-up exteriors of houses and barns,

476
00:26:00,140 --> 00:26:02,900
and it could be worth a fortune.

477
00:26:03,000 --> 00:26:06,240
Some wood is so valuable, companies like Timeless Timber

478
00:26:06,340 --> 00:26:09,940
Frames specialize in recovering and reusing it.

479
00:26:10,040 --> 00:26:12,820
They're taking apart a barn built in the 1840s

480
00:26:12,920 --> 00:26:17,060
in southern Indiana and moving it 200 miles.

481
00:26:20,160 --> 00:26:23,060
The pieces are all numbered and color-coded, then laid out

482
00:26:23,160 --> 00:26:27,000
for reassembly like a giant puzzle.

483
00:26:27,001 --> 00:26:29,000
The work crew is Amish,

484
00:26:29,001 --> 00:26:32,000
a group familiar with traditional building techniques.

485
00:26:32,001 --> 00:26:37,000
Today we are reassembling a barn that was built in the 1840s.

486
00:26:37,001 --> 00:26:40,000
These barns were built by master craftsmen

487
00:26:40,001 --> 00:26:42,000
who settled here from Europe.

488
00:26:42,001 --> 00:26:45,000
These craftsmen left marks that reveal what it took

489
00:26:45,001 --> 00:26:46,000
to carve out timbers.

490
00:26:46,001 --> 00:26:48,000
They cut each one from a log.

491
00:26:48,001 --> 00:26:52,000
Somebody that was hewing a log would score the log on top

492
00:26:52,001 --> 00:26:56,000
with a felling ax, and that log would be flipped

493
00:26:56,001 --> 00:26:58,500
and they can come on with a broad axe

494
00:26:58,600 --> 00:27:00,640
and go straight down the side of it.

495
00:27:00,740 --> 00:27:04,400
And you see that's almost a perfect 90-degree angle.

496
00:27:04,500 --> 00:27:05,640
Incredible.

497
00:27:05,740 --> 00:27:08,440
You're talking 15 to 30 work hours

498
00:27:08,540 --> 00:27:11,240
involved with just making a timber square.

499
00:27:11,340 --> 00:27:14,360
Timber framing is also known as post and beam

500
00:27:14,460 --> 00:27:17,060
because those are the two basic parts.

501
00:27:17,160 --> 00:27:21,100
The idea is to interlock large timbers that bear tons of weight

502
00:27:21,200 --> 00:27:23,760
to create a large open interior.

503
00:27:23,761 --> 00:27:27,360
The barn's frame is a basic but very solid structure.

504
00:27:27,400 --> 00:27:29,900
Four bents, four support sections,

505
00:27:29,940 --> 00:27:32,440
connected by top plates.

506
00:27:32,460 --> 00:27:35,740
The first step is to put each bent back together.

507
00:27:42,780 --> 00:27:46,980
Today we're lifting this bent section with a crane.

508
00:27:47,020 --> 00:27:50,220
Years ago, they would have used a horse and block and tackle

509
00:27:50,260 --> 00:27:53,760
or the whole neighborhood.

510
00:27:53,761 --> 00:28:00,480
This bent section probably weighs 3,000 pounds, and I wouldn't even want to think about having

511
00:28:00,481 --> 00:28:08,780
to get that many guys here to lift it by hand.

512
00:28:08,781 --> 00:28:15,660
Once the bent section is picked up and swung over in place, it'll be set down on the foundation.

513
00:28:15,661 --> 00:28:19,960
The original builders didn't use metal nails to connect the frame, and Timeless Timbers

514
00:28:19,961 --> 00:28:23,180
follows the same process.

515
00:28:23,181 --> 00:28:26,180
These two beams are held together with mortise and tenon joinery.

516
00:28:26,181 --> 00:28:28,720
This is a tenon on the post.

517
00:28:28,721 --> 00:28:33,000
There's also a tenon on this beam that comes over and goes through this mortise hole in

518
00:28:33,001 --> 00:28:34,000
the post.

519
00:28:34,001 --> 00:28:39,460
So this tenon is actually attached to this beam, and they're pulled together, and then

520
00:28:39,461 --> 00:28:41,860
they're joined with wooden pegs.

521
00:28:41,861 --> 00:28:46,680
It takes the Timeless Timbers crew less than a day to reassemble and place the

522
00:28:46,681 --> 00:28:49,980
vents.

523
00:28:49,981 --> 00:28:54,600
The next step is to connect them with a long horizontal piece called a top plate.

524
00:28:54,601 --> 00:28:59,140
It's tricky because the wood has a memory and it wants to go back to the place that

525
00:28:59,141 --> 00:29:02,740
it was prior when it was a barn for 140 years.

526
00:29:02,741 --> 00:29:05,340
So when it's moved, it'll get out of square.

527
00:29:05,341 --> 00:29:07,220
They're making adjustments to it right now.

528
00:29:07,221 --> 00:29:10,740
Sometimes it's hard to get that top plate back to where it needs to be to have everything

529
00:29:10,741 --> 00:29:13,420
line up just perfect.

530
00:29:13,421 --> 00:29:18,200
Every piece has to go back together exactly the way it came apart.

531
00:29:18,201 --> 00:29:22,700
If you look at a timber frame, you have to look at the whole thing as a system.

532
00:29:22,701 --> 00:29:27,140
When you're building a timber frame, you can only be off less than an inch on anything

533
00:29:27,141 --> 00:29:28,140
that you do.

534
00:29:28,141 --> 00:29:31,520
If it's too loose, the structure will fall apart eventually.

535
00:29:31,521 --> 00:29:34,460
If it's too tight, you're bound to break things.

536
00:29:34,461 --> 00:29:36,440
Timber framing is an art.

537
00:29:36,441 --> 00:29:42,300
It's an art that Timeless Timbers keeps alive in modern houses and artists' studios.

538
00:29:42,301 --> 00:29:46,380
For all its versatility, wood does have its weaknesses,

539
00:29:46,381 --> 00:29:49,100
rot and fire.

540
00:29:53,280 --> 00:29:55,880
Wood is so versatile, we can submerge it,

541
00:29:57,080 --> 00:30:00,020
bend it, and even reuse it.

542
00:30:01,380 --> 00:30:03,220
And it will remain as strong as ever.

543
00:30:04,560 --> 00:30:07,820
But when wood alternates between wet and dry,

544
00:30:07,821 --> 00:30:10,960
it becomes food for microbes and insects.

545
00:30:10,961 --> 00:30:14,340
There's this sailing term called sailing the bottom off,

546
00:30:14,341 --> 00:30:18,960
and it's a perfect example of the underwater wood staying good,

547
00:30:18,961 --> 00:30:20,500
the above water staying good,

548
00:30:20,501 --> 00:30:23,480
and right where those two met, rot occurred.

549
00:30:23,481 --> 00:30:26,240
The ship would literally separate

550
00:30:26,241 --> 00:30:28,840
where the waterline met the airline.

551
00:30:31,020 --> 00:30:34,060
Today, boat builders like Chesapeake Lightcraft

552
00:30:34,061 --> 00:30:36,720
try to avoid sailing their bottoms off

553
00:30:36,721 --> 00:30:39,180
by combining wood with modern technology.

554
00:30:40,960 --> 00:30:43,720
They create over 85 different boat kits

555
00:30:43,721 --> 00:30:45,740
that an average person can assemble.

556
00:30:47,060 --> 00:30:50,100
Almost all of them start out as a specialty marine plywood.

557
00:30:52,340 --> 00:30:54,300
This is a plywood called Okumi.

558
00:30:54,820 --> 00:30:57,140
It comes from Gabon in West Africa.

559
00:30:57,600 --> 00:30:59,640
It's milled there into veneers,

560
00:31:00,000 --> 00:31:02,320
and the veneers are sent to France,

561
00:31:02,321 --> 00:31:06,220
where it's made into plywood on special presses

562
00:31:06,221 --> 00:31:10,040
that use microwaves to cure the glue in the plywood.

563
00:31:10,041 --> 00:31:12,880
and put it on the CNC machine and cut boats out of it.

564
00:31:13,380 --> 00:31:15,380
At $125 per sheet,

565
00:31:16,180 --> 00:31:18,900
the cutters want to get every possible square inch from it.

566
00:31:20,140 --> 00:31:21,600
The business end is this,

567
00:31:21,720 --> 00:31:23,760
what is essentially a gigantic router.

568
00:31:24,240 --> 00:31:26,200
This quarter-inch carbide bit,

569
00:31:26,620 --> 00:31:28,080
we have to change every single day

570
00:31:28,081 --> 00:31:30,600
because of the abrasiveness of the glue in the plywood.

571
00:31:31,700 --> 00:31:34,620
It cuts at a precision of 2 thousandths of an inch.

572
00:31:40,040 --> 00:31:42,700
paired with the router, a drill creates holes

573
00:31:42,701 --> 00:31:43,980
for connecting the parts.

574
00:31:45,300 --> 00:31:47,780
The CNC machine is programmed to cut thousands

575
00:31:47,781 --> 00:31:52,700
of different parts, from whole panels to small patches.

576
00:31:56,280 --> 00:31:59,380
One of Chesapeake Lightcraft's most popular kits

577
00:31:59,381 --> 00:32:00,840
is the Cocktail Classic.

578
00:32:02,060 --> 00:32:05,040
It takes two people less than a day to assemble most of it.

579
00:32:06,540 --> 00:32:09,500
At this stage, it's very similar to a traditional boat

580
00:32:09,501 --> 00:32:11,500
prone to rot.

581
00:32:11,501 --> 00:32:14,000
But when they apply fiberglass and epoxy,

582
00:32:14,001 --> 00:32:17,000
they're adding a layer of modern materials that seals out

583
00:32:17,001 --> 00:32:19,940
water and stiffens the structure.

584
00:32:19,941 --> 00:32:25,740
We'll smooth the hole off and add a layer of fiberglass

585
00:32:25,741 --> 00:32:30,060
over the entire hull, which will multiply the strength

586
00:32:30,061 --> 00:32:35,500
of the wood and eliminate maintenance.

587
00:32:35,501 --> 00:32:37,000
The end product?

588
00:32:37,001 --> 00:32:45,000
A fast, rugged little boat that'll last for 100 years or more.

589
00:32:45,100 --> 00:32:48,040
While water can make wood vulnerable to rot,

590
00:32:48,140 --> 00:32:51,240
fire can devour it in a matter of seconds.

591
00:32:51,340 --> 00:32:54,520
In 1906, the great earthquake of San Francisco

592
00:32:54,620 --> 00:32:56,960
damaged much of that city, but it

593
00:32:57,060 --> 00:33:00,060
was a series of fires that devastated it.

594
00:33:00,160 --> 00:33:03,800
They raged for four days and caused roughly 90%

595
00:33:03,900 --> 00:33:06,160
of the destruction.

596
00:33:06,161 --> 00:33:13,000
Wood fuels fire, which is why it's the ideal material for matches.

597
00:33:13,100 --> 00:33:15,600
But early matches contained many toxic chemicals

598
00:33:15,700 --> 00:33:17,900
and produced a vile smell.

599
00:33:18,000 --> 00:33:20,400
They were known as lucifers.

600
00:33:20,500 --> 00:33:24,920
In 1910, diamond matches patented the first non-poisonous match

601
00:33:25,020 --> 00:33:26,820
in the United States, and their goal

602
00:33:26,920 --> 00:33:29,320
has been to make the safest matches possible.

603
00:33:29,420 --> 00:33:31,820
This is where all the process begins.

604
00:33:31,920 --> 00:33:34,260
The logs that you see on the dock here,

605
00:33:34,261 --> 00:33:36,260
This would be about one day's production.

606
00:33:36,261 --> 00:33:39,260
They use aspen because it's strong, straight,

607
00:33:39,261 --> 00:33:44,260
easy to machine, and most importantly, it burns well.

608
00:33:44,261 --> 00:33:46,260
This is called the bull chain.

609
00:33:46,261 --> 00:33:48,260
The bull chain is what brings the logs into the plant

610
00:33:48,261 --> 00:33:50,260
and starts the debarking process.

611
00:33:50,261 --> 00:33:53,260
The logs are all about 100 inches long.

612
00:33:53,261 --> 00:33:56,260
They're debarked.

613
00:33:56,261 --> 00:34:00,260
And then we cut them into lengths of about 24 to 25 inches.

614
00:34:00,261 --> 00:34:02,260
The logs are loaded into a steam bin

615
00:34:02,261 --> 00:34:05,260
to loosen the fibers and kill bacteria.

616
00:34:05,261 --> 00:34:08,260
The next thing they do is go to the lathe,

617
00:34:08,261 --> 00:34:10,260
which starts the veneering process.

618
00:34:10,261 --> 00:34:13,260
A rotary lathe spins the wood,

619
00:34:13,261 --> 00:34:16,260
while a wide blade peels the log into long, flat sheets.

620
00:34:18,260 --> 00:34:21,260
The operator feeds stacks of sheets into a chopper.

621
00:34:24,260 --> 00:34:26,260
Here, the sheets are cut into splints.

622
00:34:28,260 --> 00:34:31,260
The chopper uses two connected sets of blades,

623
00:34:31,261 --> 00:34:36,700
sets of blades and cuts like a high-speed double guillotine.

624
00:34:36,701 --> 00:34:39,060
The splints are now matchsticks.

625
00:34:39,061 --> 00:34:42,360
Diamond wants them to burn, but not for too long.

626
00:34:42,361 --> 00:34:45,060
If the wood creates an ember, it could start a fire when

627
00:34:45,061 --> 00:34:46,460
tossed out.

628
00:34:46,461 --> 00:34:48,920
So Diamond runs them through a bath of monoammonium

629
00:34:48,921 --> 00:34:51,720
phosphate.

630
00:34:51,721 --> 00:34:54,820
The wood will still catch fire, but when it's blown out,

631
00:34:54,821 --> 00:34:57,860
its embers die almost immediately.

632
00:34:57,861 --> 00:35:00,500
Once dry, the splints are pressed together to face

633
00:35:00,501 --> 00:35:04,180
the same direction. This gets them ready to be forced into a chain.

634
00:35:05,460 --> 00:35:12,560
What's happening behind us is the splint with the wooden stick is getting set in a hole on this

635
00:35:12,561 --> 00:35:17,527
chain above us. Misshapen and broken splints fall out and are discarded.

636
00:35:17,528 --> 00:35:19,320
The next step is to put on

637
00:35:19,321 --> 00:35:20,536
the match head.

638
00:35:20,537 --> 00:35:27,020
Old match heads contain chemicals that made both maker and user sick. Today it's

639
00:35:27,021 --> 00:35:29,360
dipped into a non-poisonous mix,

640
00:35:29,361 --> 00:35:31,520
containing potassium chlorate and sulfur.

641
00:35:32,860 --> 00:35:34,580
The match is complete,

642
00:35:34,581 --> 00:35:36,700
but there's one more safety measure.

643
00:35:36,701 --> 00:35:38,740
The chemicals in the head won't light

644
00:35:38,741 --> 00:35:40,640
unless they're run across a rough surface

645
00:35:40,641 --> 00:35:42,260
containing red phosphorus.

646
00:35:43,120 --> 00:35:45,820
Diamond prints this material right onto its boxes.

647
00:35:47,480 --> 00:35:50,840
Loaded into the boxes, the matches are ready for shipping.

648
00:35:50,841 --> 00:35:54,800
We produce approximately 38 million matches a day.

649
00:35:54,801 --> 00:35:56,740
The number of matches that we produce a year,

650
00:35:57,620 --> 00:35:59,700
we figured out if you line them up end to end,

651
00:35:59,780 --> 00:36:02,820
they would go around the Earth at the equator about 11 times.

652
00:36:03,900 --> 00:36:09,160
That's 273,917 miles of matches every year.

653
00:36:12,880 --> 00:36:15,360
From the tiny to the mighty,

654
00:36:16,180 --> 00:36:18,060
trees come in all shapes and sizes.

655
00:36:19,000 --> 00:36:20,440
In the United States alone,

656
00:36:20,540 --> 00:36:22,660
there's over 1,200 different species of tree.

657
00:36:22,960 --> 00:36:24,040
On a worldwide basis,

658
00:36:24,041 --> 00:36:26,580
some people will go as high as 40,000 to 50,000

659
00:36:26,581 --> 00:36:28,280
different species of trees.

660
00:36:28,281 --> 00:36:31,480
Oak alone, there's 200 different species of oak.

661
00:36:31,481 --> 00:36:34,480
Trees have adapted to nature in 1,000 different ways,

662
00:36:34,481 --> 00:36:36,380
and we've taken those characteristics

663
00:36:36,381 --> 00:36:39,480
and used those trees to adapt to our needs.

664
00:36:39,481 --> 00:36:41,060
Our greatest adaptation of wood

665
00:36:41,061 --> 00:36:46,500
could be when we peel it, put the layers back together,

666
00:36:46,501 --> 00:36:49,260
and you've got flywood.

667
00:36:49,261 --> 00:36:51,800
At Oregon State University, they spent a century

668
00:36:51,801 --> 00:36:55,300
figuring out how to get the most from wood.

669
00:36:55,301 --> 00:36:57,500
Wood can be peeled out like this,

670
00:36:57,501 --> 00:37:00,000
but the grain is still going in this direction.

671
00:37:00,001 --> 00:37:05,940
You can make use of that by taking wood in this direction,

672
00:37:05,941 --> 00:37:11,460
but then plying it with wood that you turn 90 degrees

673
00:37:11,461 --> 00:37:13,060
and put them together like this.

674
00:37:13,061 --> 00:37:15,060
And it's like a steel-belted radial.

675
00:37:15,061 --> 00:37:18,760
You end up having strength going in alternating planes

676
00:37:18,761 --> 00:37:22,960
that gives the wood strength not only in this direction,

677
00:37:22,961 --> 00:37:26,800
downward, but also laterally, which you wouldn't get in wood

678
00:37:26,801 --> 00:37:28,800
naturally. Don't think of plywood

679
00:37:28,801 --> 00:37:31,000
as made from leftover scraps.

680
00:37:31,001 --> 00:37:33,280
It's more like a composite material.

681
00:37:33,281 --> 00:37:37,640
Plywood is a good example of us emulating wood's natural

682
00:37:37,641 --> 00:37:39,920
strength and actually taking advantage of it

683
00:37:39,921 --> 00:37:42,760
and doing one better. Humans have been gluing layers

684
00:37:42,761 --> 00:37:45,960
of wood together since ancient times, especially where wood

685
00:37:45,961 --> 00:37:48,760
was scarce. The Egyptians created

686
00:37:48,761 --> 00:37:52,760
They would just slice wood very thin, alternate the layers,

687
00:37:52,761 --> 00:37:55,760
and they used it for a lot of the things found in the tombs.

688
00:37:55,761 --> 00:37:58,760
But modern plywood came about in the 1800s

689
00:37:58,761 --> 00:38:01,760
with the invention of a rotary lathe.

690
00:38:01,761 --> 00:38:05,760
It spins the log while a blade peels it into thin layers,

691
00:38:05,761 --> 00:38:08,760
like on rolling paper towels.

692
00:38:08,761 --> 00:38:13,760
This lathe can turn a large log into a mile-long piece of ply.

693
00:38:13,761 --> 00:38:16,760
The greatest plywood construction of all time

694
00:38:16,761 --> 00:38:21,000
of all time was an enormous boat that could fly.

695
00:38:21,100 --> 00:38:25,260
It was the brainchild of the eccentric genius, Howard Hughes.

696
00:38:25,360 --> 00:38:27,700
He called it the Hercules, but it's

697
00:38:27,800 --> 00:38:33,280
better known by a nickname Hughes hated, the Spruce Goose.

698
00:38:33,380 --> 00:38:36,040
Hughes did nothing halfway, and the Spruce Goose

699
00:38:36,140 --> 00:38:38,520
was pure Hughes.

700
00:38:38,620 --> 00:38:42,760
Today, it's housed in Oregon's Evergreen Aviation Museum.

701
00:38:42,860 --> 00:38:45,860
The aircraft is the largest in the world

702
00:38:45,861 --> 00:38:48,020
by wingspan.

703
00:38:48,021 --> 00:38:51,200
It's 319 feet and 11 inches.

704
00:38:51,201 --> 00:38:55,400
It's about 80-some feet tall, weighs about 300,000 pounds,

705
00:38:55,401 --> 00:38:59,600
and could carry another 100,000 pounds.

706
00:38:59,601 --> 00:39:02,620
Its wings are about the length of a football field,

707
00:39:02,621 --> 00:39:05,620
and it was over 10 times bigger than the Army's main troop

708
00:39:05,621 --> 00:39:10,320
transport plane, the C-47.

709
00:39:10,321 --> 00:39:14,360
Hughes began work on the Spruce Goose in 1942.

710
00:39:14,361 --> 00:39:18,600
During World War II, German U-boats were sinking so many ships,

711
00:39:18,700 --> 00:39:22,740
the US was afraid of sending out troop transports.

712
00:39:22,840 --> 00:39:24,200
Hughes' goal?

713
00:39:24,300 --> 00:39:26,200
Fly over the U-boats.

714
00:39:26,300 --> 00:39:29,640
He set out to build a plane that could carry 750 fully

715
00:39:29,740 --> 00:39:30,980
equipped men.

716
00:39:31,080 --> 00:39:34,420
But U-boats weren't the only obstacle Hughes faced.

717
00:39:34,520 --> 00:39:36,920
He was told he could not use strategic materials,

718
00:39:37,020 --> 00:39:40,080
such as aluminum or magnesium, and so on,

719
00:39:40,180 --> 00:39:42,960
because those were being used for fighters and bombers.

720
00:39:42,961 --> 00:39:46,960
so he was forced to look at other sources of material,

721
00:39:46,961 --> 00:39:50,960
and wood was the major available source for doing this.

722
00:39:50,961 --> 00:39:54,700
Hughes assembled a team of engineers and furniture makers.

723
00:39:54,701 --> 00:39:57,700
They used plies of wood peeled from birch trees

724
00:39:57,701 --> 00:39:59,100
and molded the layers.

725
00:39:59,101 --> 00:40:01,420
They called it dura mold.

726
00:40:01,421 --> 00:40:04,320
Everything you see in here from front to back

727
00:40:04,321 --> 00:40:06,320
to the tip of the tail is dura mold,

728
00:40:06,321 --> 00:40:08,320
which is made out of birch wood.

729
00:40:08,321 --> 00:40:11,320
Everything here is wood.

730
00:40:11,321 --> 00:40:14,520
Some sections were paper thin, while others were as thick

731
00:40:14,620 --> 00:40:16,420
as beams.

732
00:40:16,520 --> 00:40:18,320
Once the plane was glued together,

733
00:40:18,420 --> 00:40:22,060
they pulled out the nails, all eight tons of them.

734
00:40:22,160 --> 00:40:25,040
Just about the only metal is in the engines and hydraulic

735
00:40:25,140 --> 00:40:27,100
and electrical systems.

736
00:40:27,200 --> 00:40:29,880
Critics had a field day, lampooning the project

737
00:40:29,980 --> 00:40:32,500
as the flying lumber yard and questioning

738
00:40:32,600 --> 00:40:36,020
if it could even take off.

739
00:40:36,120 --> 00:40:40,380
But in 1947, Hughes had the last laugh.

740
00:40:40,381 --> 00:40:43,880
He brought the press along for what he said would just be a test of the engines.

741
00:40:44,840 --> 00:40:47,680
So he took the aircraft out, fired it up.

742
00:40:49,340 --> 00:40:52,640
He asked his co-pilot for 10 degrees of flaps.

743
00:40:52,641 --> 00:40:57,900
For a throttle, it's 60, it's about 665, it's 70.

744
00:40:58,240 --> 00:41:01,860
He hit 70 miles an hour. The airplane took off just as advertised.

745
00:41:03,120 --> 00:41:06,920
And I believe we are airborne. We are airborne, ladies and gentlemen.

746
00:41:07,380 --> 00:41:08,900
We were really up in the air.

747
00:41:10,380 --> 00:41:15,380
Vindicated, Hughes built a special hangar for the plan

748
00:41:15,381 --> 00:41:17,380
and spent a million dollars every year

749
00:41:17,381 --> 00:41:19,380
to keep it flight-ready.

750
00:41:19,381 --> 00:41:22,380
It was a monument to both his genius and his eccentricity.

751
00:41:22,381 --> 00:41:25,380
It is also a monument to wood because of the fact

752
00:41:25,381 --> 00:41:28,380
that the dura mold had incredible strength.

753
00:41:28,381 --> 00:41:31,380
We measure it every month, and the wings haven't sagged

754
00:41:31,381 --> 00:41:34,380
even a sixteenth of an inch in 10 years.

755
00:41:34,381 --> 00:41:37,380
Any way you slice it...

756
00:41:37,381 --> 00:41:40,080
you slice it.

757
00:41:40,180 --> 00:41:44,820
There's only one material that can just about do it all.

758
00:41:44,920 --> 00:41:45,920
Wood.

759
00:41:46,020 --> 00:41:48,620
There's wood in some pretty unexpected places.

760
00:41:48,720 --> 00:41:51,220
A lot of people think of it as something that's old fashioned,

761
00:41:51,320 --> 00:41:53,520
but it's been with us every step of the way

762
00:41:53,620 --> 00:41:56,840
from mankind's step out of the cradle of civilization

763
00:41:56,940 --> 00:41:58,840
right up through today.

764
00:41:58,940 --> 00:42:01,780
Strong enough to pile drive,

765
00:42:01,880 --> 00:42:06,540
flexible enough to bend, and light enough to fly.

766
00:42:06,541 --> 00:42:10,380
Wood is one of the most versatile materials we have,

767
00:42:10,381 --> 00:42:12,580
making it a modern marvel.

